    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6398\n\nSPONSOR: SERRANO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to implement a constitutional amendment author-\nizing the placement of public utility lines and bike paths in the forest\npreserve and establishing a health and safety land account\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to approve the land transactions related to\nthe implementation of the constitutionally-created forest preserve\nhealth and safety land account.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill approves the 1,250 acres of land to be conveyed\nto the State for inclusion in the Forest Preserve, to fulfill the obli-\ngations of the constitutional amendment creating the health and safety\nland account.\n\nSection 2 of this bill requires the Attorney General to approve the\ndeeds and lands described in the legislation.\n\nSection 3 of this bill describes the lands to be conveyed to the State,\nwhich includes an approximately 214 acre parcel in Ulster County and an\napproximately 1206 acre parcel in Essex County.\n\nSection 4 of this bill provides that this bill would take effect imme-\ndiately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2017, the voters approved an amendment to Article XIV, Section 1 of\nthe State Constitution, authorizing the creation of a 250-acre Health\nand Safety Land account from Forest Preserve Land, to be used for bridge\nand highway safety, highway relocation and wells for drinking water. The\namendment and its implementing legislation, as set forth in Title 21 of\nArticle 9 of the Environmental Conservation Law (ECL), provides that\nbefore such land may be used by municipalities in the Adirondacks and\nCatskills, not less than 250 acres must be acquired by the State for\ninclusion in the Forest preserve, subject to legislative approval.\n\nThis legislation details the lands that will be added to the forest\npreserve as required by the Constitutional amendment and Title 21 of\nArticle 9 of the ECL. The first parcel is located in the Catskills in\nthe Town of Olive in Ulster County, consisting of approximately 214\nacres of land, and the second parcel is located in the Adirondacks in\nthe Town of Moriah in Essex County, consisting of approximately 1206\nacres of land. These two parcels total approximately 1,420 acres,\nexceeding the constitutional amendment's 250-acre requirement.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is new legislation.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
